Hall of Great Strength
Front-facing programme (main entrance side)
ICON₁ — Śākyamuni Buddha (釋迦牟尼佛, Thai-style) (Central front altar)
POSITION    : central, axial, elevated
SCALE       : dominant
ORIENTATION : front-facing (toward main entrance)
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional, pedagogical, cosmological
The Śākyamuni image is in the Thai bronze sculptural tradition — distinguished from Chinese Buddhist Śākyamuni images by its more elongated body proportions, the specific arrangement of the uṣṇīṣa and flame-like hair, and the simplified meditative robe drape. The plaque 大雄寶殿 identifies the hall canonically; the secondary plaque 人天教主 (Rén Tiān Jiào Zhǔ, "Teacher of Humans and Devas") identifies Śākyamuni's specific epithet — the historical Buddha as the teacher of all beings in the human and divine realms. This pedagogical framing is significant: the front-facing programme presents Śākyamuni primarily in his identity as teacher, the source from which the Dharma was transmitted into the present world.

ICON₂ — Left Attendant (likely Ānanda, 阿難) ICON₃ — Right Attendant (likely Mahākāśyapa, 迦葉) (Flanking Śākyamuni)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : inward-facing (toward Śākyamuni)
FUNCTION    : pedagogical, devotional
The two standing attendant figures flanking the central Buddha are the canonical paired disciples — Ānanda (the younger, associated with verbal transmission of the sūtras) and Mahākāśyapa (the older, associated with mind-to-mind transmission). Their function and relations to Śākyamuni follow the same logic established in the Lian Shan Shuang Lin Mahavira Hall analysis.

ICON₄, ICON₅ — Paired Budai-Maitreya figures (Foreground, threshold zone of front altar)
POSITION    : peripheral, paired-lateral, threshold
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : devotional, apotropaic
Two small dark wooden Budai-Maitreya figures with raised arms are visible in the foreground, positioned at the threshold zone of the altar. Their joyful raised-arm posture is characteristic of the welcoming Budai form. As paired threshold figures, they perform a role analogous to the child attendants documented at Lian Shan Shuang Lin's Avalokiteśvara hall — admitting the devotee into the principal sacred space with iconographic warmth. Their commemorative-devotional function is distinct from the principal Buddha's pedagogical function.

Back-facing programme (rear entrance side)
ICON₆ — Standing Amitābha (阿彌陀佛, Welcoming Form / 接引佛) (Central rear altar)
POSITION    : central, axial, elevated
SCALE       : dominant
ORIENTATION : outward-facing (toward back entrance)
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional, intercessory
The standing Amitābha is shown in his canonical welcoming form: standing rather than seated (encoding active descent rather than meditative stillness), holding prayer beads in one hand, with the other hand in a welcoming-receiving gesture. This iconographic form is specifically associated with the moment of death in Pure Land devotional theology — Amitābha descends from the Western Pure Land to receive the dying soul and conduct it to rebirth in his Buddha-field. The plaque 彌陀慈父 ("Amitābha, Compassionate Father") emphasizes the paternal-receiving aspect of his role: the dying or deceased devotee is gathered into Amitābha's compassionate care.

ICON₇, ICON₈ — Possibly Avalokiteśvara and Mahāsthāmaprāpta (Lateral figures visible in Images 3 and 4)
Visible in Images 3 and 4 are at least one crowned bodhisattva figure (likely Avalokiteśvara or Mahāsthāmaprāpta) and additional smaller figures. If the configuration is the canonical Western Triad, then the back-facing programme is:
Western Triad (rear-facing):
  Amitābha (centre, standing welcoming form)
  Avalokiteśvara (lateral, compassion)
  Mahāsthāmaprāpta (lateral, wisdom)
The reflections in the glass and the lighting make precise identification difficult from these photographs; closer fieldwork observation would confirm whether the full Western Triad is present or whether the lateral figures are differently identified. The analysis treats the Western Triad as the most likely configuration pending confirmation.

